/* ======================================================================
 * SWOS_DOS_TACTICS — the DOS release's formation coverage
 * ======================================================================
 *
 * WHAT: the SWOS 96/97 DOS release re-tuned three of the twelve built-in
 * formations. Nine are byte-identical to the Amiga's; 4-4-2 differs in 68 of
 * its 350 coverage bytes, Sweep in 64 and 4-3-3 in 10 — 142 of 4200 in all.
 * 3-4-3 needs a variant here only because THIS PORT changed it (see
 * SWOS_ORIGINAL_TACTICS above); the DOS 3-4-3 is the pristine Amiga one.
 *
 * The 4-4-2 and Sweep deltas are not the nudges the byte counts suggest: 60 of
 * the 4-4-2's 68 and 61 of Sweep's 64 are the two FORWARD slots, rewritten
 * rather than adjusted — DOS keeps the strikers in a narrow central channel
 * where the Amiga spreads them wide. Everything outside those two slots is
 * single-byte changes, one per slot.
 *
 * WHY: `ApplyGameStyle` (dos_gameplay.c) switches the physics and timer
 * constants for DOS gameplay but not the formations, and `GetPlayerPrice`
 * walks the tactics table for every player — so DOS mode was deriving Amiga
 * player values, and through AdjustPlayerSkills, Amiga in-game skill nibbles.
 *
 * HOW: build with -DSWOS_DOS_TACTICS. Baked from
 * bin/data_json/tactics/<id>_dos.json by scripts/emit_tactics.py, which
 * round-trip checks them like everything else; the data was extracted from the
 * DOS disassembly (swos-port/swos/swos.asm `tact_*`, whose g_tacticsTable order
 * is ours) and matches docs/tactics_amiga_vs_dos/tactics_dos.json byte for byte.
 * Mutually exclusive with SWOS_ORIGINAL_TACTICS. `make clean` first — the flag
 * changes baked data (CLAUDE.md).
 *
 * CAVEAT, and it is a real one: the recorded-game corpus (docs/TESTS.md,
 * "The recording grades our formation data") DISAGREES with the two forward
 * slots. Fifteen of the eighteen 4-4-2/Sweep forwards in the corpus match the
 * AMIGA rows and none match these, while every discriminating byte in slots 0-7
 * matches DOS. So this is faithfully what the DOS binary holds, and it is NOT
 * what produced those recordings. Turning it on scores 225/240 on the corpus's
 * own player values where the shipped data scores 238/240.
 *
 * The two name-byte differences are cosmetic and carried for fidelity: the
 * 9-byte name is "442\0 B" style — a label, a NUL, then a grade letter the
 * tactics menu shows. DOS grades 4-4-2 "B" where the Amiga says "A" (and
 * writes no space), and 4-3-3 "B" where the Amiga says "C".
 * ====================================================================== */
/* Always compiled, not `#ifdef SWOS_DOS_TACTICS`: DOS-gameplay mode selects
 * these at runtime (`TacticsData_SelectRelease`, called from ApplyGameStyle),
 * because the corpus showed the formations are part of what --dos-gameplay
 * means. ~1.5 KB of data on both targets. The compile-time flag still exists
 * and still pins the build to this set; see the block above. */
